  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist.\n\n**Data exploration:** The ES|QL and search endpoints aren't exposed on this Kibana, so I couldn't run aggregations. I worked from the data view's field mapping instead. The fields that matter here are `response.keyword` (status codes), `clientip`, `bytes`, `geo.dest`, `extension.keyword`, and `timestamp` as the time field.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-92c3304d Web logs overview\" (id `eff-92c3304d-web-logs-overview-80b627a2b747`) has six panels:\n- Total requests (count)\n- Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Average response bytes\n- Requests over time (line, date histogram on `timestamp`)\n- Responses by status code (pie, top 10 `response.keyword`)\n- Top destination countries (bar, top 10 `geo.dest`)\n\nI set the dashboard's time range to the last year because I couldn't check the sample data's time span. Adjust it if the data falls outside that window.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-92c3304d 5xx spike\" (id `ffc98caa-8332-4986-bffd-e9ed95baee25`) is a `.es-query` rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`:\n- Counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5*` over the last 5 minutes, using `timestamp`.\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Runs every 1m.\n\nChoices I made:\n- **Enabled:** I created the rule enabled so it actually runs. It has no action connector, so it won't notify anyone; it only reports status. Disable it with `disable_alert_rule` if you don't want it running.\n- **Tag:** I tagged the rule `EFF-92c3304d`.\n- **Status:** The rule's status is `ok`.",
